 to quite easily the  entire trojan war you've got  some gods lined up on one side some gods  lined up on another  the same thing goes for the odyssey  where poseidon  um you know he's not happy with with  odysseus to begin with  but then when odysseus manages to pluck  out the eye of his son  and uh tells them hey it's me that did  it  his son prays to him and poseidon now  doesn't want to let odysseus go home  meanwhile athena wants to let him go  home  so we've got this this uh motif that  comes up  over and over again of conflict between  the gods about particular things  and in this case euthyphro is actually  bringing a lawsuit  prosecuting his father and he's  he's you know coming into the court as  the accuser  but he's also coming into the court as  you might say a defendant  in public opinion because everybody's  looking at him and saying  it's wrong for you it's wrong  not only in a way having to do with  justice but in a way having to do with  holiness  for you to be prosecuting your father  and  if he wanted to look at you know classic  examples  zeus takes action against his father his  own father takes action against him  um so there's plenty of precedent there  and euthyphro is drawing on that  socrates is going to point out a big  problem with this  if you're going to say that doing the  right thing the pious thing  is what's pleasing to the gods then when  you've got this greek religion which is  polytheistic that means they have  multiple gods who you know  are in charge of different things and  and no one of which  actually can control the entire  operation  even zeus who's the king of the gods can  be fooled can be circumvented  these are also anthropomorphic gods that  means that they are like human beings  in that they have feelings desires  plans and these are these don't all line  up with each other  socrates then is able to say well this  definition of the pious that you're  giving me is not particularly useful  because what about cases where the gods  actually disagree with each other now  notice how important  this anthropomorphism is  in in dealing with this issue because if  there were multiple gods but they were  unlike human beings let's say they were  more like  bees where there's a queen and the queen  really is in charge of everything  and all the workers you know more or  less are  following her lead whatever that happens  to be we don't really  fully understand how these things work  but just imagine that sort of case  then it wouldn't be that big of a  problem because you wouldn't have  disagreement arising  and if there was disagreement there  would be ways that it could be resolved  but  human beings with human beings in human  affairs disagreement  works somewhat differently as we'll see  in just a moment  so if the gods really are like super  powerful human beings that have  all sorts of you know other attributes  and have been around  for a very long time and get to metal  and human affairs  then they're going to disagree with each  other along the same lines that human  beings do so we can we can figure out  what that's going to be like by looking  at the way that human beings disagree  with each other  socrates brings up two different domains  in which people would find themselves in  disagreement  and one of these is what we might call  the mathematical domain  and we could extend this to include  anything where there's some sort of  decision procedure in place  where there is a clearly defined canon  of how you in fact  take care of these things if we wanted  to think of something more  qualitative and not quantitative we  could think about  cuisine you know chefs actually have you  know when there's a recipe  there's certain things that have to go  into the recipe there's a flavor profile  you taste things quite a bit you can  disagree you know are these  these eggs bad or not does this have  enough cinnamon or not  did you cook this the right way or not  but  you can figure out the answers to that  one person is going to be right and the  other person is going to be wrong  perhaps both are wrong because some  other person is right there's a standard  so in matters like that we can  end up in disagreement but we end up in  disagreement because  somebody has got a mistaken perspective  and that person can be  led to see if they're not you know just  being stubborn or  stupid that they have a mistaken  perspective  so you know i think that my car takes  unleaded  and my friend thinks that it takes  diesel somehow he manages to jam the  diesel into there even though it's not  shaped for that fills it up for a diesel  car doesn't run right  you know i can say look at dummy i told  you it took  unleaded and you know here's the manual  now where can't we do that sort of thing  or where does that break down and  notice the other index that he has where  do we get  super excited passionate  angry even hateful with each other  well it's in what we call  moral matters with numbers if you  think that you know this video is 20  minutes long and i think that it's 10  minutes long we can look at the youtube  uh reading for it or we could look at  the actual raw data and we could say  well it's  this amount of time you're right i'm  wrong or i'm right you're wrong  when it comes to moral matters this  doesn't mean by the way that socrates  thinks this is all up in the air that  nobody's right that everybody's right or  anything like that  he's just saying we do often disagree  about moral matters  what are moral matters the just and the  unjust or sometimes you'll see it  translated as the right and the wrong  the the kion and adico  the good and the bad the agathon and  cocoon and this has a moral sense to it  and there's multiple  ways in which things can be good or bad  in other platonic dialogues you'll often  see him referencing the pleasurable  and the painful usually in order to say  well that's not really what good and bad  means so  what he often means is the advantageous  and the harmful  or just the morally good and bad in a  way that's not  simply reducible to the just and the  unjust  and then we have this other catch-all  category that covers an  awful lot that the greeks did feel to be  the realm of moral qualities and i think  we probably ought to see it that way too  the noble and the base or the beautiful  and the ugly or the honorable and  dishonorable or  shameful the colon and  icecrown and these are the sorts of  things that people do in fact disagree  about  go to any you know  holiday gathering give some people some  some liquor  and throw some controversial issues out  of the table and you're gonna have  people arguing with each other  unless you have extremely well  disciplined people  uh go to thanksgiving you know and see  the student who's come home from college  and now  sees things in a different light than  their parents do  once again we're going to have argument  now what's really interesting about this  and socrates uses the example of a court  of law  as a prime case  disagreement is not so easy to settle  when it comes to these kinds of matters  why not well there isn't  one single decision procedure  or universally recognized standard or  criterion  that we can appeal to like if you know  if we've got a mistake in numbers we've  got  mathematics as a discipline if um  you think that the the  house should go over there and it's in  the middle of a flood plain and the  engineer says not a bad idea  or if you're going to do that you better  get flood insurance  again pretty easy to resolve isn't that  does somebody have a right to build a  house where they want to well you know  we could look at the laws and see what  the laws say but even when we're  applying laws in a very  uh strict and unemotional  you know sort of disinterested sense  there can sometimes be disagreements  about just what the law means  what would actually be the right thing  the just thing in this the situation  but quite often when people are  in some sort of conflict with each other  they  are not seeing eye to eye  not only about whether something is in  fact just or unjust  but oftentimes about what the just and  the unjust  actually are so not just about the  particulars but even about  the general the universal  they may not even have a solid idea  about what the just or the unjust is  and we often do confuse our own  interests for what is just  in other people's for what is unjust  likewise good and bad  when it comes to beautiful or ugly or  think of you know aesthetic  qualities like uh somebody being a  stand-up guy and somebody being a  complete douchebag those would be the  sort of things that would actually fall  under noble and base  for the ancient greeks and  we do have a hard time coming to terms  about these we can provide explanations  we can achieve some sort of consensus  but we very easily do fall into  quite often acrimonious disagreements  conflicts agonistic you know  back and forth arguments about this sort  of thing  so if we can do it the fact that the  gods are conceived  of an anthropomorphic way in a  polytheistic way  means that this can happen with the gods  as well that constitutes a problem  for figuring out just what this is  because  if we're talking about what is pleasing  to the gods  what the gods like what the gods  think has these qualities and not these  qualities  then you're going to get some god saying  i like x  and other gods saying i hate x and  what is a mortal to do then how do you  decide  what actually counts as the holy thing  or the pious thing  to do you're pleasing some gods and  displeasing others  becomes impossible to decide at that  point except in some sort of arbitrary  you know maybe flip a coin or i'll just  go with apollo all the time sort of way  and that clearly is not going to work

